<div class="hero-inner">
<p class="kicker">A Whitetail Story</p>
<h1>Wait Till He Turns</h1>
<p class="dek">They went to fill feeders. Nathan brought the .35 Whelen anyway.</p>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span>Nathan Deville</span>
<span>November 8, 2025</span>
<span>Latanier → Lena, Louisiana</span>
</div>
</div>
</header>
<div class="article-content">
<p class="context">Earlier that morning, before they left, the sun was orange and yellow with pink and purple in it.</p>
<p class="context">Nathan Deville and his dad left Latanier at 7:20.</p>
<p class="context">On a hunting day they would have been gone by five, in place at daylight, 6:30. This was not that kind of day. They were headed to their hunting lease in Lena to fill feeders. His dad had just bought a Honda Ranger, a side by side, and they wanted to see what terrain it could go through.</p>
<p class="context">It was a relatively cold morning. Most of the leaves on the trees were brown. The green had nearly all fallen.</p>
<div class="section-space"></div>
<p class="voice">Something told me to bring a rifle, just in case there was the slim chance we saw something.</p>
<p class="voice">I brought my <strong>.35 Whelen.</strong></p>
<p class="voice">An astronomically large round. Nine times out of ten, it can drop them there.</p>
<p class="voice">We filled all the feeders. When we were done, we had time to blow. So we sat in a stand for a little bit, relaxed and enjoyed the scenery.</p>
<p class="voice">The ground was dry. With all the pine trees, there were shadows everywhere. It smelled almost completely of pine, with a natural earth smell underneath.</p>
<p class="voice">For about ten minutes, me and Dad were laughing, hollering, yelling, hitting stuff. Just having a good time.</p>
<p class="voice"><strong>Then I looked up.</strong></p>
<p class="voice">On the right side of my shooting lane, a buck had come out. It froze for a second. Then it started following the right treeline toward us.</p>
<p class="voice">It was around 9:15. I was the only one who saw it. I gave Dad the signal that one had come out. We both froze.</p>
<p class="voice">I had never shot a deer with someone there, let alone my dad. My heart was pounding.</p>
<aside class="pull">
<p>Wait...wait...wait till he turns so you can broadside him.</p>
<span class="attribution">Dad</span>
</aside>
<p class="voice">I waited.</p>
<p class="voice">It came up to about 120 yards. Then it turned broadside. I could see its ribs and its side.</p>
<p class="voice">I held for about ten seconds, on the heart. Then I pulled the trigger.</p>
<section class="moment">
<p class="moment-label">The shot</p>
<p class="moment-number">116 yards</p>
<p class="moment-copy">The buck dropped where it stood.</p>
</section>
<p class="voice">He dropped. No running. No struggle. He went down where he stood.</p>
<p class="voice">That was when I realized, in full length, what had just happened.</p>
<p class="voice">Dad got extremely excited. He couldn't believe it. We had just been in the same stand laughing, shouting, hitting stuff, having a good time, and then that happened.</p>
<p class="voice">We gave him a quick skim and couldn't find an entrance or exit hole. I aimed at the heart. That's what I know.</p>
<p class="voice">It was an eight point. The biggest buck I have shot to date.</p>
<p class="voice">We don't keep track of score or spread, just points and health.</p>
<div class="ending">
<p class="voice">I think it will most likely be the best deer I ever kill.</p>
<p class="voice">My dad was right there beside me, telling me what to do and when to do it.</p>
<p class="voice"><strong>It was my most priceless and unforgettable hunt.</strong></p>
</div>
